<p class="lead">Investors who analyze every lead by hand can only look at a handful a day. Real estate deal analysis software takes an address and returns comps, a repair estimate and offer prices for each strategy, so you spend your time on the deals worth pursuing.</p>
<h2 id="what-manual-underwriting-involves">What manual underwriting involves</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Pulling property records and recent sales nearby.</li>
  <li>Choosing which sales are true comparables, and adjusting for differences.</li>
  <li>Estimating repairs and the after-repair value.</li>
  <li>Running the numbers for each exit strategy.</li>
  <li>Writing it up for a partner or a seller.</li>
</ul>
<p>Done well, that's 30 to 45 minutes per property.</p>
<h2 id="the-automated-flow">The automated flow</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Enter an address.</li>
  <li>Pull data: property records and recent sales from a data provider such as ATTOM, or MLS data through the <a href="/blogs/reso-web-api-mls-integration">RESO Web API</a> if you have access.</li>
  <li>Score comps: each sale is scored on distance, age, size, condition and date, with the reason shown.</li>
  <li>Estimate value and repairs: from the chosen comps and your repair assumptions.</li>
  <li>Run every strategy at once: wholesale, fix and flip, subject-to and seller finance, each with its own offer price.</li>
  <li>Export: a summary and a PDF to share.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="keep-the-math-out-of-the-ai">Keep the math out of the AI</h2>
<p>Language models are not reliable at arithmetic, and an offer price has to be exact and repeatable. In our build, every number comes from normal code that gives the same answer every time. The AI only reads the result and explains it in plain language: why these comps, what drives the price, what to check on site. That makes every number easy to audit.</p>
<h2 id="what-changes-in-practice">What changes in practice</h2>
